-# Temporary-account provider proposal
+# Temporary-account provider-browser broker: implementation plan
 
-A backend browser broker could allow provider callbacks without a permanent
-mobile registry entry. It would issue a new short-lived provider-browser handoff
-credential, correlate browser completion to temporary identity/expiry, and add
-a server return endpoint. A mobile callback compatibility adapter could then
-share the broker across all providers and permanent accounts.
+## Scope lock and outcome
 
-Putting this behind `TEMP_PROVIDER_BROKER` would allow staged rollout. A follow-on
-could replace native provider OAuth entirely. This is a proposal, with migration,
-credential purpose/replay rules, and rollback still unresolved.
+The user explicitly approved this expansion: a temporary-account provider-browser
+broker, a purpose-scoped handoff credential, and its compatibility boundary with
+native mobile OAuth. The approval does not authorize replacing the permanent
+native flow, changing `MobileOAuth.complete`, deployment, provider revocation, or
+production code in this task. `DESIGN.md` remains the current product contract;
+this document is the implementation plan and approval record.
+
+The target behavior is: a temporary account can complete provider linking through
+a backend browser return path, then hand the result to a client through a
+short-lived, single-use credential. Permanent accounts continue to use the
+existing registered-subject native callback and their stored grants.
+
+## Ownership and boundary
+
+* `accounts.py` remains authoritative for temporary identity and its 24-hour
+  lifetime. The implementation must set `expires_at = creation_time + 24h`; the
+  current constructor does not yet do so.
+* A new `TemporaryProviderBroker` owns browser transactions, provider callback
+  correlation, temporary provider-link state, and handoff issuance/consumption.
+  It must not write the permanent registry.
+* `MobileOAuth` remains authoritative for the permanent native callback. Its
+  current permanent-subject guard remains in force for every provider.
+* The compatibility adapter is a narrow boundary: it accepts only a broker
+  handoff for a temporary account and produces the temporary-link result. It
+  never passes that credential to the native callback or upgrades it into an
+  ordinary product session.
+* The composition root selects the broker only for temporary accounts and only
+  when `TEMP_PROVIDER_BROKER` is enabled. Provider and storage details stay
+  behind the broker adapters.
+
+## Handoff contract
+
+Persist a broker transaction keyed by an opaque random `transaction_id`, with
+`temporary_account_id`, provider, fixed purpose `temporary_provider_link`, fixed
+audience `mobile_temporary_link`, issued/expiry times, state, and `consumed_at`.
+Store only a hash of the bearer token. The token carries no provider grant or
+account data and must have a short lifetime (for example, 10 minutes), bounded
+by the temporary account expiry.
+
+The broker validates audience, purpose, account, provider, expiry, account
+lifecycle, and transaction state at browser return and again immediately before
+handoff consumption. Consumption is an atomic compare-and-set that records
+`consumed_at`; retries receive an explicit already-consumed response. Expired or
+deleted accounts revoke future writes, and a late callback cannot recreate the
+account or link. Provider failures, invalid state, expired credentials, replay,
+and account deletion have distinct, non-success outcomes without echoing token
+material.
+
+The native callback and broker use disjoint callback/credential types. A native
+callback cannot consume a broker handoff, and a broker handoff cannot establish a
+permanent registry subject or modify a permanent grant. This is the compatibility
+fence for old mobile clients: they continue to send the existing native callback;
+only clients that understand the broker result use the adapter.
+
+## Expiry and privacy lifecycle
+
+The transaction expiry is `min(account.expires_at, issued_at + handoff_ttl)`. At
+temporary-account expiry, revoke write authority first, mark the transaction and
+link state expired/deleted, and then remove temporary provider material. A
+lazy check on every read/write is required even if a cleanup sweep is added.
+Cleanup must be idempotent and retain a minimal tombstone or equivalent
+compare-and-set fence for the period in which delayed callbacks could arrive.
+
+The account identifier, provider, transaction metadata, token hash, callback
+logs, and failure telemetry inherit the temporary account's purpose and
+retention. Logs must not contain bearer tokens, grants, callback query values, or
+unbounded provider payloads. Define the retention duration and whether a
+provider has already received data before implementation; external provider
+copies cannot be erased by local cleanup. Buffered telemetry and retries must
+check the lifecycle fence so expiry cannot recreate state.
+
+## Migration and rollout cost
+
+This is an additive migration. No permanent-account rows or existing grants are
+converted, and no backfill is needed. Add a versioned broker transaction/link
+representation and additive readers/writers. During coexistence, old clients
+remain native-only while broker-capable clients use the temporary adapter. Gate
+only temporary-account admission with `TEMP_PROVIDER_BROKER`, initially disabled,
+then enable by provider/client cohort after synthetic and integration evidence.
+
+Operational cost includes a bounded transaction store, token-hash lookup and
+single-use fencing, expiry cleanup/tombstones, metrics for issued/expired/
+consumed/replayed transactions, and provider error classification. Migration
+must account for transactions in every state, pending callbacks, expired
+accounts, and rollback binaries that can still see the additive records. No
+record may be silently interpreted as a permanent registry entry.
+
+## Rollback cost and procedure
+
+Rollback is a configuration disable of `TEMP_PROVIDER_BROKER`, followed by
+stopping new temporary broker admissions. Existing native permanent linking is
+unchanged and remains available throughout. In-flight broker transactions may
+complete only if the old binary can safely reject them; otherwise expire them at
+their bounded TTL and surface a retry path. Do not delete transaction rows while
+callbacks can still arrive: retain the tombstone/expiry fence through the maximum
+TTL, then purge hashed tokens and temporary link material with an auditable
+cleanup. Any provider grants already disclosed externally require provider-side
+revocation or must be recorded as an external-erasure limitation. Re-enable is
+safe only after schema readers and lifecycle fences are confirmed compatible.
+
+## Implementation slices and proof
+
+1. Add the 24-hour temporary-account expiry invariant and broker transaction
+   representation, with deterministic clock-based tests.
+2. Implement browser start/return correlation and provider error mapping; test
+   callback duplication, timeout, invalid state, process loss, and provider
+   failure.
+3. Implement purpose/audience validation and atomic single-use handoff; test
+   replay, account switch, permission/capability loss, expiry between check and
+   use, and deletion between stages.
+4. Add the temporary-only compatibility adapter and prove that permanent native
+   callbacks and stored grants are byte-for-byte behaviorally preserved.
+5. Add bounded cleanup, lifecycle-safe telemetry, cohort metrics, and the feature
+   flag; exercise disable/rollback with pending transactions and old-client
+   requests.
+
+Before implementation, run changed-path security and privacy assurance over the
+broker, adapter, credential, expiry, logs, and delayed writers. The assurance
+review is self-review unless an independent reviewer is available; it must leave
+any unavailable coverage explicit. The implementation owner proof is synthetic
+and local; real provider/device and production rollout evidence remain pending.
+
+## Open decisions before coding
+
+The handoff TTL, transaction-store technology, provider grant retention, exact
+temporary-link storage shape, cleanup cadence, client capability signal, and
+external provider-revocation contract are unresolved. They must be decided and
+recorded before implementation; none changes the approved boundary above.
